cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use IML, please cite it as below."
title: "IML (I-Lang Machine Layer)"
abstract: >-
  IML (I-Lang Machine Layer) is an experimental machine form of I-Lang v4.x
  operation chains, with fixed-width codes derived from the I-Lang canon at a
  pinned commit. Version 0.2 fixes the supported subset (one linear operation
  chain), the registry derivation and its digest, the grammar and its
  segmentation rule, the message header, the canonical I-Lang print, the
  round-trip law at the AST level, and six error codes taken from the I-Lang
  specification, and ships a reference codec, golden and malformed corpora and
  a measurement report. Version 0.3 changes the surface only: ASCII marks
  (@ for a target or an entity reference, $ for OUT, one space between
  operations) and a document form that carries one header for many chains;
  the registry, the AST, the round-trip law and the error codes are unchanged.
  Version 0.4 adds the two flow forms the I-Lang canon defines for operation
  chains, the batch shorthand [BATC:VERB] and multi-line chains, and records
  that conditionals, parallel groups, DAGs, error handling and retry are not
  operation-chain syntax in the canon and therefore not in IML.
  IML registers no verb, modifier key, entity or declaration of its own and is
  not part of the I-Lang canon.
